WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:03.600
Have you ever wondered
if there’s a natural way

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.160
to lower your high blood pressure,
guard against Alzheimer's,

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:11.440
lose weight, and feel better?
Well, it turns out there is!

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:13.680
Welcome to the
NutritionFacts Podcast.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.920
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:20.120
The mind is a terrible
thing to waste,

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:23.800
so today we look at ways to
improve our cognitive function

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:27.080
and we start with a study
that measured the relationship

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:31.080
between exercise and
dementia prevention.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:33.680
Based on a meta-analysis
of 20 studies,

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:35.360
mental performance
may be improved

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:38.840
with even modest weight loss
among overweight individuals,

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:41.680
though that may be due
not to the weight loss per se,

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:44.400
but rather
the prescribed exercise.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.560
For example, one study
randomized obese elders

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:48.560
to one of four groups
for a year:

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:50.480
a weight-loss diet alone,

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:53.720
exercise without weight loss,
both, or neither.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:56.360
Compared to the control group
that did neither,

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:59.680
global cognition improved
in all the three other groups,

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:01.520
but while weight loss
and exercise

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:03.360
beat out weight loss alone,

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:06.480
weight loss and exercise
didn't beat the exercise alone,

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:10.240
suggesting that exercise is
the most potent component.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:12.200
The American College
of Sports Medicine

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:15.480
cites meta-analyses of dozens
of prospective studies

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:17.360
following tens of thousands
of people for years

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:19.520
that suggest
that those who are active

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:22.640
have a 38 percent lower risk
of cognitive decline

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:24.200
and a 39 percent lower risk

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:26.720
of developing
Alzheimer's disease.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:29.960
And, apparently,
the more, the better.

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:32.840
Every extra 500 calories
expended a week

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:34.560
appears to correlate
with an additional

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:37.280
ten percent drop
in dementia risk.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:39.240
However, even light
physical activity,

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:41.280
such as low-intensity walking,

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:43.520
or how yoga and tai chi
are often practiced

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:45.400
by older adults,
has been associated

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:47.520
with at least
some sort of cognitive gain

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:50.080
in about half of the studies
done to date.

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:51.800
If the relationship
between exercise

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:54.640
and dementia prevention
is cause and effect,

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:56.880
as much as 20 percent
of all cases

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:58.720
of Alzheimer's disease
in Western countries

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:02.040
may be attributable
to physical inactivity.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:04.880
That would mean more than
a million cases of Alzheimer's

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:06.720
just in the U.S. alone.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:09.120
As with all observational data,
though,

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:11.400
there's the potential
for confounding

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:14.000
or reverse causation.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.360
Maybe there's a shared factor
causing both,

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:17.880
such as atherosclerosis,

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:20.240
contributing to both inactivity
and dementia,

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:22.640
or maybe those
genetically predisposed

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:23.560
to exercise a lot

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:26.240
also have a more robust
cognitive reserve

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:28.320
(though a Mendelian
randomization study

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:30.000
failed to find this).

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.880
Instead of inactivity
leading to dementia,

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:35.640
dementia could lead
to inactivity.

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:37.640
Prospective studies
following people over time

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:41.200
can show that the inactivity
does precede the dementia,

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.040
but both mental
and physical activity

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:46.440
can start declining
years before a diagnosis.

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:48.360
When researchers looked closely
at the data,

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:50.400
only inactivity
less than 10 years

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:53.400
before the diagnosis
was associated with dementia,

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:57.440
suggesting the causality
may indeed be backward.

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:00.800
When put to the test,
interventional trials show

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:02.240
physical activity
can be increased

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:03.520
in dementia patients,

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:06.080
which can improve
physical fitness,

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:10.320
but disappointingly did not
slow cognitive impairment.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:13.640
Perhaps, we're just not
catching people early enough?

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:15.400
Adults with either
normal cognition

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:17.000
or mild cognitive impairment

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:18.560
randomized to exercise training

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:20.360
tend to improve
their cognition

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:21.360
compared to those ending up

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:23.560
in the less active
control groups.

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:25.080
A slight majority of studies

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:27.360
that concurrently tracked
brain imaging

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:29.040
found those randomized
to exercise

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:31.000
experienced a beneficial effect

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:32.240
on overall brain volume

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:34.680
as well as specifically
the hippocampus,

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:36.360
a part of the brain
that plays a critical role

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:39.280
in memory formation
and for which atrophy predicts

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:41.480
memory decline and dementia.

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:44.040
The seminal study,
"Exercise training

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:47.120
increases size of hippocampus
and improves memory,"

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:49.600
randomized older adults
to either a year

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:52.720
of supervised moderate
aerobic exercise (walking)

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:55.200
or a control group,
which instead involved a year

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:57.520
of stretching
and toning classes.

00:03:57.520 --> 00:03:59.400
On average,
the hippocampus volume

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:02.800
of those in the control group
shrank 1.4 percent,

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:03.640
which is consistent

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:05.600
with the one to two percent
annual shrinkage

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:07.400
typical of old age.

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:08.440
In contrast,

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:10.600
over that same year
in the walking group,

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:13.280
their hippocampus volume
increased two percent,

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:16.160
which is like adding
a year or two of brain volume

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:18.760
(though may subsequently shrink
back to baseline

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:21.200
in as few as six weeks
of inactivity;

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:22.520
so you got to keep it up).

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:24.560
A similar study
that added a third group-

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:26.440
a resistance training arm-

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:29.040
found a similar
aerobic training reversal

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:31.240
of age-related shrinkage
in the memory centers

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:34.000
compared to the balance
and toning control group,

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:35.640
but unfortunately
there was no benefit seen

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:38.640
in the nonaerobic
strength-training group.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:42.240
It's unclear exactly which type
of exercise is best,

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:45.160
but the cognitive benefit
of aerobic training

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:48.360
appears to be larger
than resistance training.

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:50.120
In most of the randomized
controlled trials

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:51.040
of older adults,

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:54.920
walking was the most frequently
chosen mode of exercise.

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:57.680
The best volume, duration,
frequency, or intensity

00:04:57.680 --> 00:04:59.720
is also unknown.

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:00.760
Based on a meta-analysis

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:03.440
of nearly a hundred
randomized controlled trials,

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:05.920
more important
than session duration,

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:09.200
weekly frequency,
program duration, or intensity,

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:12.240
may be total training time.

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:15.160
However studies spliced it up,
it appeared to take exercising

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:18.280
for a total
of at least 52 hours

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:22.360
before a cognitive benefit
can be established.

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:23.120
In our next story,

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:27.800
we look at non-drug, non-supplement,
non-lifestyle approaches

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:29.640
to dementia prevention
and treatment.

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:32.360
In my book, How Not to Age,

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:34.520
I discuss the role
of drugs and supplements

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:36.800
and focus on diet
and lifestyle approaches

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:40.400
to the prevention and treatment
of cognitive decline,

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:43.480
but there are a few common
non-drug, non-supplement,

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:46.240
non-lifestyle approaches
to dementia treatment.

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:48.760
For example,
cognitive stimulation therapy,

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:50.440
which typically involves
group activities

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:52.000
and social interaction.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:55.120
There have been at least 44
randomized controlled trials

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:56.360
of cognitive stimulation

00:05:56.360 --> 00:05:58.960
including thousands
of participants.

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:02.880
ADAS-Cog, short for Alzheimer's
Disease Assessment Scale

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:04.120
Cognitive subscale,

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:06.200
is the most commonly used
measure of cognition

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:08.280
in clinical trials
of dementia treatment.

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:09.920
A clinically relevant
improvement

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:13.000
is often defined as a reduction
of four or more points

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:15.120
on the ADAS-Cog measure.

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:17.720
Regrettably, the attenuation
of cognitive decline

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:20.120
for most of the cognitive
stimulation studies

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:22.720
averaged only about two points.

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:24.560
Now you could argue,
look, any improvement

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:26.120
is better than nothing,

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:27.760
but unfortunately,
the cognitive effects

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:29.360
don't appear to generalize

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:31.920
to helping
with everyday life situations.

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:33.200
You can train people
to get better

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:35.600
at a computerized "brain game,"

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:39.640
but that doesn't necessarily
translate to other arenas.

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:42.160
Also, the marginal improvements
are temporary.

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:43.520
They don't seem to persist

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:45.960
once the intervention period
is over.

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:47.680
While cognitive
stimulation therapy

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:50.240
may have no significant
overall effect

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:51.920
on quality of life,

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:54.160
any kind of group therapy
has been shown

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:57.000
to improve
psychological well-being.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:58.320
As social creatures,

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:00.680
social participation is
important for mental health

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:02.240
throughout the life course,

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:03.960
but there's
insufficient evidence

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:07.680
that it can improve or prevent
cognitive dysfunction.

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:10.640
Dementia is associated
with social isolation,

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:12.880
but this may be
reverse causation,

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:15.000
where the dementia leads
to social withdrawal,

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:17.760
instead of the other way around.

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:19.680
The same issue
comes up all the time

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:20.520
with those advocating

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:23.120
for "use-it-or-lose-it"
mental stimulation

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:25.000
to forestall decline.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:26.520
For example, this review

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:28.760
in a peer-reviewed
aging journal suggested

00:07:28.760 --> 00:07:31.200
that "if a person
solves crosswords

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:32.560
four times a week,

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:36.080
they can reduce their risk
of dementia by 47 percent."

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:38.320
Yes, doing
frequent crossword puzzles

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:41.560
is associated with lower
subsequent dementia risk,

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:42.680
but isn't it more likely

00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:45.120
that those on the verge
of a dementia diagnosis

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:47.520
are less likely to be doing
crossword puzzles,

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:50.640
as a result of their declining
cognitive abilities?

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:52.920
Music therapy presents
a similar story

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:54.520
of improving well-being

00:07:54.520 --> 00:07:57.240
without necessarily
affecting cognition.

00:07:57.240 --> 00:07:59.040
Even in late stages
of dementia,

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:01.080
when verbal communication
is lost,

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:03.640
music can be enjoyed.

00:08:03.640 --> 00:08:06.600
However, it does not seem
to improve brain function.

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:08.520
Most (five out of seven)
meta-analyses

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:10.920
of the 40+ interventional
clinical trials

00:08:10.920 --> 00:08:12.760
of music therapy
for people with dementia

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:15.480
found no cognitive benefits.

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:16.600
And the few studies that did

00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:18.160
found the effect
was short-lived,

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:20.720
disappearing
after one to three months.

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:23.320
Even though cognition
doesn't seem to be affected,

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:24.800
there still may be
positive effects

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:26.680
in terms of reducing agitation

00:08:26.680 --> 00:08:29.520
and improving mood
and socialization.

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:33.480
One of the kookiest treatments
I ran across was cryotherapy,

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:35.920
also known as cryostimulation.

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:37.840
Those with mild
cognitive impairment

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:39.520
randomized
to short-term exposures

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:43.920
to extremely cold temperatures
experienced a transient boost

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:46.280
in general
cognitive functioning

00:08:46.280 --> 00:08:49.960
(no longer apparent two weeks
after daily sessions ended).

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:52.360
Study subjects in shorts
and a T-shirt

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:55.320
were exposed to 58 degrees
below zero (-50 C)

00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:56.760
for two minutes.

00:08:57.640 --> 00:09:01.000
No, wait, that was
the placebo control group

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:04.320
exposed to "noncryogenic"
temperatures.

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:07.000
How low
did the experimental group go?

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:07.960
Two minutes

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:13.640
at more than 200 degrees
below zero (-130 C).

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:16.000
